WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.250 --> 00:00:02.349
How do archaeologists know how

2
00:00:02.349 --> 00:00:03.848
old something is?

3
00:00:03.848 --> 00:00:06.375
One answer comes from carbon.

4
00:00:06.713 --> 00:00:08.892
The Danish curator Thomsen ordered

5
00:00:08.892 --> 00:00:10.316
the past into Stone,

6
00:00:10.316 --> 00:00:12.137
Bronze, and Iron Ages.

7
00:00:12.137 --> 00:00:13.776
The Stone Age splits into

8
00:00:13.776 --> 00:00:16.772
Palaeolithic, Mesolithic and Neolithic.

9
00:00:17.120 --> 00:00:21.161
Willard Libby developed radiocarbon dating

10
00:00:21.161 --> 00:00:22.300
in 1949.

11
00:00:22.300 --> 00:00:24.796
Carbon-14 has a half-life of

12
00:00:24.796 --> 00:00:26.573
about 5,730 years,

13
00:00:26.573 --> 00:00:28.815
so it works for roughly

14
00:00:28.815 --> 00:00:30.471
50,000 years.

15
00:00:30.815 --> 00:00:32.666
Tree rings date wood.

16
00:00:32.666 --> 00:00:35.930
Potassium–argon dates volcanic layers millions

17
00:00:35.930 --> 00:00:37.482
of years old.

18
00:00:37.806 --> 00:00:39.077
Your turn.

19
00:00:39.077 --> 00:00:40.994
What is the half-life?
